WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.360
Operational technology monitors and controls physical processes.

00:00:04.360 --> 00:00:16.413
That connection makes OT security a matter of safety, reliability, production, environmental protection, and continuity of essential services, not only data protection.

00:00:16.413 --> 00:00:22.311
OT and IT share technology but operate under different constraints.

00:00:22.311 --> 00:00:34.365
Long equipment lifecycles, strict availability requirements, specialized protocols, maintenance windows, and physical consequences require security controls to be tested against operational reality.

00:00:34.365 --> 00:00:41.802
IT practices remain valuable when they are adapted with engineering and operator input.

00:00:41.802 --> 00:00:57.958
Strong OT programs understand their assets and communication paths, segment unnecessary connectivity, secure remote access, monitor networks, control privileges, maintain recoverable backups, manage vulnerabilities, govern changes, and practice incident response.

00:00:57.958 --> 00:01:05.908
Architecture and controls should reduce credible operational risk without undermining the process they protect.

00:01:05.908 --> 00:01:09.755
No team can accomplish this alone.

00:01:09.755 --> 00:01:15.653
Security professionals contribute threat awareness, monitoring, identity, and incident-handling expertise.

00:01:15.653 --> 00:01:23.091
Engineers and operators contribute process knowledge, safety constraints, and authority over operational changes.

00:01:23.091 --> 00:01:28.476
Vendors, management, and risk owners also have defined responsibilities.

00:01:28.476 --> 00:01:32.323
The central takeaway is disciplined collaboration.

00:01:32.323 --> 00:01:41.812
Know the operation, protect trustworthy control and visibility, make connectivity intentional, validate changes, and prepare for recovery.

00:01:41.812 --> 00:01:49.762
OT security is strongest when cybersecurity and engineering work as one operational risk-management function.
